blican president leaves the GOP without an incumbency advantage", "No general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices yet, keeping uncertainty wide", "Primary-season headlines (Markey-Moulton, Buttigieg enthusiasm, Rubio among Massachusetts Republicans) do not yet bear on the general"], "rationale": "Massachusetts' fourteen-point Democratic lean and the absence of a Republican incumbent give Democrats a clear fundamentals-based edge, and no polls or ratings justify moving away from it. Primary-season coverage of the Democratic field does not yet change the general-election picture.", "watch": "Early statewide general-election polling, the emergence of a strong Republican nominee, or the top-two primary initiative could shift the outlook.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Massachusetts is among the most Democratic states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of fourteen points toward Democrats. The 2028 race is an open contest: the incumbent Republican president is term-limited, so the GOP cannot rely on incumbency advantages. Attention so far has centered on the Democratic primary, where polls show Ed Markey narrowly ahead of Seth Moulton and Democratic voters expressing enthusiasm for Pete Buttigieg, while a separate survey found Massachusetts Republicans most excited about Marco Rubio.\n\nNo general-election polls, expert ratings, or prediction-market prices are available yet, so the outlook rests on the state's fundamentals. That picture points to a clear but not certain Democratic advantage, with wide uncertainty until actual campaign polling arrives. The race currently sits in Likely Democratic territory.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9396, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p